    <comment>New package: libsigmf 1.0.2 (Apache-2.0)

libsigmf is DeepSig's C++ implementation of SigMF, the Signal Metadata
Format: the JSON sidecar convention describing a set of recorded digital
signal samples -- sample rate, capture segments, annotations, and the
extension namespaces (antenna, spatial, signal, ADS-B, WiFi) layered on
top. It reads and writes those .sigmf-meta records.

Why Factory: SigMF is the interchange format for recorded RF captures,
and this is its reference C++ implementation. It is packaged here as part
of maintaining the SDR stack in hardware:sdr, where an SDRangel Factory
submission is also being prepared separately -- that submission has not
been accepted, and nothing here depends on it. Debian ships this as
libsigmf.

Packaging notes for review:

* Header-only. Upstream declares add_library(libsigmf INTERFACE) and
  compiles nothing, so there is deliberately no lib&lt;name&gt;&lt;N&gt; runtime
  subpackage and no soname split -- only libsigmf-devel, carrying the
  headers, the FlatBuffers schemas and the CMake package config. The
  built RPM contains no .so and no .a.

* Nothing is bundled. Upstream carries nlohmann/json and flatbuffers as
  git submodules; both are empty directories in the release tarball. The
  build sets -DUSE_SYSTEM_JSON=ON -DUSE_SYSTEM_FLATBUFFERS=ON and builds
  against Factory's nlohmann_json and flatbuffers, so no bundled()
  provides are warranted. The generated FlatBuffers headers are
  regenerated by the system flatc rather than shipped from upstream's
  stale pre-generated copies, so they match the flatbuffers runtime.

* One patch, libsigmf-install-interface-include-path.patch: the installed
  CMake interface exposed only &lt;prefix&gt;/include, so upstream's own
  documented usage, #include &lt;sigmf.h&gt;, did not compile. This is upstream
  commit 3bc4be190368, unreleased since 1.0.2; Debian carries the same
  change. It should be dropped when upstream next tags.

* %check runs upstream's three self-contained example programs (one is a
  full JSON round-trip) plus a throwaway consumer project configured
  against the staged tree, so a break in the installed CMake package
  config is caught at build time rather than in every consumer. The
  examples are built with -UNDEBUG deliberately: their asserts are the
  entire verification, and under CMake's default RelWithDebInfo NDEBUG
  they would report success unconditionally.

* Upstream cadence, stated plainly: v1.0.2 is the latest tag and dates
  from 2022-12-14. The project is not archived and took 8 commits in
  January 2026, but has cut no release in over three years. This submits
  the released tag rather than a snapshot.</comment>
